  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Thunderstorm chances increase this week, but timing \nand intensity remain uncertain.\n\nThe focus of showers and thunderstorms today has now trended south \nof our area. Abundant mid-level cloud cover really prevented a lot \nof instability from developing and temperatures have been several \ndegrees below forecast. The warmest conditions in Central VA where \ntemps are near 90F. The forecast was updated to lower rain chances, \nwith coverage of thunderstorms now expected to be low and focused in \nCentral VA to southern MD. Any lingering showers dissipate this \nevening, with muggy and mostly cloudy skies tonight.\n\nLooking at the rest of this week, the strong mid-level ridge \npositioned over the Mid-South will slowly push east toward the TN \nValley. Numerous shortwave troughs / disturbances will round the top \nof the ridge, traversing the Great Lakes to Mid-Atlantic. This is \ngoing to bring us several rounds of thunderstorms, some of which \ncould be strong to severe. The presence of strong lift and \nincreasing shear, along with a hot and unstable airmass will produce \nan environment supporting of significant severe thunderstorms. The \nbig caveat is timing. This setup can be very tricky to forecast \nexactly when these complexes of storms develop, and exactly where \nthey track. The next shortwave is forecast to arrive Tuesday \nafternoon/evening. SPC has the northeast part of the forecast area \nin a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5), and a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) \ndown into central VA. The latest 12Z guidance today has pushed back \nconvection to Tuesday evening, and by that time we may avoid the \nworst of the severe weather impacts as storms arrive after peak \ndaytime heating. \n\nSPC also has a Slight Risk across the area Wednesday through Friday \nas several additional shortwave troughs are expected to traverse the \nregion. At some point this week, most likely toward the end when a \nstrong upper trough digs south from Canada, we will be in a much \nmore favorable position for the upper forcing, instability, and \ndaytime heating to align. The latest AI/ML guidance indicates the \nhighest chance for this is now on Thursday, but I would not discount \nany day for potentially strong to severe thunderstorms at this time. \n\nA cold front begins to slide south through the area Friday night \ninto Saturday, bringing drier conditions and seasonal temperatures. \nSome thunderstorm chances linger south of I-66 on Saturday, then \nmost of the area is dry and cooler come Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Near-record early September heat expected.\n\nA strong ridge of high pressure over the southern Plains will \nbuild toward the Mid-South throughout this week. While humidity \nwill be up to support several rounds of showers and thunderstorms, \nthe heat will be up as well, especially Tuesday through Thursday.\nHigh temperatures are expected to reach the lower to middle 90s \nTuesday and Wednesday, while making a possible run at 100 degrees in \na few spots in parts of eastern and northeastern Virginia, near the \nmetros, and along the eastern areas of the Blue Ridge Mountains on \nThursday. Heat indices, because of the humidity, will be high as \nwell. Heat indices should reach 100 to 103 degrees in scattered \nplaces in interior parts of our region on Tuesday. Expect more \nwidespread 100 to 105 degree heat indices on Wednesday. To round it \noff, Thursday will have heat indices readings more so in the 103 to \n105 degree ranges. These ranges can obviously change some by a few \ndegrees warmer or a few degrees cooler. Either way, the point we \nwant to make is it is going to be hot and humid through much of the \nweek. Heat headlines could be issued soon regarding this 3-day \nstretch of heat and humidity. For the moment, monitor information at \nwww.weather.gov. Even Friday, although may be cooler in terms of \ntemperatures because of convection with a front, heat indices could \nstill reach the 95 to 100 degree range in many places.",
